\image(figures/damerau_script.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The inserted byte is placed between the endpoints that were just transposed. Each script position refers to the current evolving string. No manual edit sequence is substituted for the core traceback.)
\alttext(Three boxes read CA, AC and ABC. The first arrow says transpose at zero and the second insert at one. A note contrasts unrestricted distance two with OSA three.)

> Define the distance before choosing a recurrence

Every insertion, deletion, substitution of a different byte and adjacent
transposition costs one; matching equal bytes costs zero. The unrestricted
distance d_DL(a,b) is the minimum total cost of a script taking a to b.
Damerau's historical correction work motivates the four operation classes;
Lowrance and Wagner supply the general multiple-edit algorithm used here.
No population frequency or claim that transposition is the most common error
is needed to define this mathematical model.

The edit graph immediately explains the metric properties. Identity costs
zero, and a different string needs at least one positive-cost operation.
Every operation has a same-cost inverse, giving symmetry. Concatenating an
optimal a→b script with an optimal b→c script is an admissible unrestricted
script from a to c, so

\[
d_{DL}(a,c)\le d_{DL}(a,b)+d_{DL}(b,c),\qquad
\bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|\le d_{DL}(a,b)\le d_L(a,b)\le\max(|a|,|b|).
\]
\alttext(Unrestricted edit distance satisfies triangle inequality, is at least the byte-length difference, and is no greater than Levenshtein distance or the longer byte length.)

Only insertions and deletions change length by one. For the upper bound,
substitute the shorter common prefix as needed, then insert/delete the length
difference. Every Levenshtein script is also an unrestricted script, proving
d_DL≤d_L. Strict improvement means that some script using transposition beats
every script without it; it need not be described solely as replacing two
substitutions. The CA→ABC witness also includes an insertion. A pair differing
only by a swap of two unequal adjacent bytes has DL=1 and Levenshtein=2;
swapping equal bytes leaves the string unchanged and costs distance zero.

> Retain the original five pairs and compute all three measures

OSA adds only the local crossed-byte prefix move O(i−2,j−2)+1 to the ordinary
Levenshtein recurrence, when the two ending bytes cross-match. It forbids
reusing an aligned region in the way unrestricted scripts permit. The new
native OSA API is a comparison method; the original unrestricted function
keeps its name and semantics. All five original pairs are evaluated below,
including the case-sensitive CA/ABC distinction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/damerau_levenshtein.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::string>> pairs{{"ab","ba"},{"abcd","abdc"},{"receive","recieve"},{"data","adta"},{"CA","ABC"}};
  std::vector<double> indices,lev,osa,dl;std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| original source | original target | Levenshtein | OSA | unrestricted DL |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<pairs.size();++k) {
    const auto& [a,b]=pairs[k];const auto l=dm::algorithms::levenshtein_distance(a,b),o=dm::algorithms::optimal_string_alignment_distance(a,b),d=dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_distance(a,b);
    if(l!=(k<4?2:3) || o!=(k<4?1:3) || d!=(k<4?1:2)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",a,b,l,o,d);indices.push_back(k);lev.push_back(l);osa.push_back(o);dl.push_back(d);labels.push_back(a+"/"+b);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.points(indices,lev,"Lev",{220,38,38},10).points(indices,osa,"OSA",{234,88,12},7).points(indices,dl,"DL",{37,99,235},4)
      .legend({"Lev","OSA","DL"},{{220,38,38},{234,88,12},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Original five pairs: local OSA and unrestricted DL differ")
      .x_ticks(indices,labels).x_label("source/target byte strings").y_label("unit edit distance")
      .x_limits(-.5,4.5).y_limits(0,4.5).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/damerau_levenshtein.svg");
  std::print("\nAll five original Levenshtein/DL rows retained; OSA is computed rather than assumed.\n");
}
