\caption(The ordinary prefix grid omits the finite-marker border only for presentation; the complete native trace retains it. A valid two-edit unrestricted script demonstrates why OSA's direct value three violates triangle inequality.)
\alttext(A three by four grid has rows zero-one-two-three, one-one-two-two, and two-one-two-two. Its green final two is explained by a last-occurrence bridge costing zero plus zero plus one plus one.)

The traceback uses first minima in diagonal, insertion, deletion, bridge
order. It is deterministic, but need not enumerate every optimal script.
For a selected bridge, native script expansion deletes the source interior
at the evolving position, swaps its adjacent endpoints and inserts the target
interior. Processing prefix blocks left to right maintains a completed target
prefix followed by the unconsumed source suffix. This invariant explains why
current-state positions remain valid even when preceding edits change length.
Matches produce no instruction; the number of emitted unit edits equals the
native unrestricted distance. Local shape, crossed-byte and recurrence checks
protect recovery from malformed public records, without claiming that arbitrary
edited tables are still globally optimal.

> Measure rectangular work and account for the borders

For source length n and target length m, the recurrence evaluates exactly nm
interior cells and one byte-equality test per cell. Table initialization and
borders still cost work when either string is empty. This full-table kernel
uses (n+2)(m+2) size_t entries plus 256 last-source-row entries. The trace adds
one compact candidate record per interior cell; it stores no evolving-string
snapshots. Time and space are O((n+1)(m+1)+256), including empty cases. OSA
instead needs three rolling rows of length min(n,m)+1 for its local recurrence;
that space advantage does not give it the unrestricted metric's semantics.

The next square and rectangular controls use all-a sources and all-b targets.
Every cell is evaluated, even though no equal-byte or eligible bridge event
occurs. Doubling only the target length doubles the interior work. The table
and edit caps below reject complete requests rather than truncating evidence.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/damerau_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colours{{37,99,235},{234,88,12}};
  std::print("| source bytes | target bytes | complete table cells | actual interior updates | byte matches | eligible bridges | distance |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(int factor:{1,2}) {
    std::vector<double> lengths,work;
    for(std::size_t n:{8,16,32,64,128}) {
      const std::size_t m=factor*n;const auto t=dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace(std::string(n,'a'),std::string(m,'b'));
      if(t.work.cells_evaluated!=n*m || t.work.byte_comparisons!=n*m || t.work.byte_matches || t.work.eligible_transpositions || t.distance!=m) return 1;
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,m,t.table.size(),t.work.cells_evaluated,t.work.byte_matches,t.work.eligible_transpositions,t.distance);
      lengths.push_back(n);work.push_back(t.work.cells_evaluated);
    }
    plot.line(lengths,work,factor==1?"m = n":"m = 2n",colours[factor-1],2.5).points(lengths,work,"",colours[factor-1],4);
  }
  plot.legend({"m = n","m = 2n"},colours).title("Actual interior updates equal source length times target length")
      .x_label("source bytes n").y_label("actual cell updates").x_limits(0,135).y_limits(0,43000).size(1000,560)
      .save_svg("build/damerau_work.svg");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace("CA","ABC");int rejected=0;
  try {dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace("CA","ABC",19);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++rejected;}
  try {dm::algorithms::damerau_edit_script(t,1);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++rejected;}
  try {dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace("",std::string(100,'a'),203);} catch(const std::length_error&) {++rejected;}
  try {dm::algorithms::apply_byte_edits("CA",{{dm::algorithms::DamerauMove::erase,0,"X",""}});} catch(const std::invalid_argument&) {++rejected;}
  if(rejected!=4) return 1;
  const auto empty=dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace("",std::string(100,'a'),0);
  const auto binary=dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_trace(std::string("\0A",2),std::string("A\0",2));const auto edits=dm::algorithms::damerau_edit_script(binary,0);
  const bool replayed=dm::algorithms::apply_byte_edits(binary.source,edits)==binary.target;
  if(binary.distance!=1 || !replayed) return 1;
  std::print("\nCell cap, edit cap, empty-border cap and expected-byte mismatch: {} expected rejections. Empty/100-byte case has {} table cells and {} interior updates.\n",rejected,empty.table.size(),empty.work.cells_evaluated);
  std::print("NUL-containing adjacent swap: distance {}, replay matches target {}. UTF-8 encoded e-acute to ASCII e: byte distance {}.\n",binary.distance,replayed,dm::algorithms::damerau_levenshtein_distance(std::string("\xc3\xa9",2),"e"));
}
