Cell cap, edit cap, empty-border cap and expected-byte mismatch: 4 expected rejections. Empty/100-byte case has 204 table cells and 0 interior updates.
NUL-containing adjacent swap: distance 1, replay matches target true. UTF-8 encoded e-acute to ASCII e: byte distance 2.
\image(figures/damerau_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The two curves count the executing recurrence, excluding allocation and traceback. Border cells remain part of the complete cap, even when there are zero interior updates.)
\alttext(Two rising curves end at 16384 updates for 128 by 128 bytes and 32768 for 128 by 256 bytes.)

The trace's default complete cell cap is 1,000,000, including the marker and
prefix borders; it is checked before input copying, table/record allocation
or computation. Script output has a separate 100,000-instruction cap, checked
after basic shape/distance validation and before traceback/output allocation.
Zero disables the corresponding optional cap; checked geometry and container
limits remain. Candidate sums clip safely at the checked n+m bound. Counter
overflow rejects rather than wrapping, and no trace/script silently truncates.
The legacy scalar signature and string_metrics include remain available,
without an optional allocation cap. Other string metrics are unchanged.

Byte positions are literal std::string offsets. Embedded NULs and every byte
value are valid, with no UTF-8 decoding, case folding, normalization or
grapheme segmentation. The encoded accented character in the final control
occupies two bytes and therefore has byte distance two to ASCII e. This is
the declared alphabet, rather than a Unicode character-distance computation.
Replay validates each operation's current position, exact expected bytes and
payload shape on a private by-value string; failures leave an lvalue caller
unchanged. Generic std::string replacements can cost O(kL+n) for k edits and
maximum evolving length L, so script replay is a separate cost from computing
the distance or expanding its O(n+m+distance) traceback.

The permanent independent oracle performs breadth-first shortest-path search
using literal insert/delete/substitute/adjacent-swap neighbours, without any
prefix-DP recurrence. All 121 ternary strings through length four give 14,641
pairs. Every target distance is at most four, so a search limited to four
edits and intermediate length source_length+4 is complete; projecting all
outside bytes to a fixed ternary byte never increases cost, so outside-
alphabet intermediates offer no shorter route.
Native distances, literal script replay and three-row OSA versus a full-table
OSA control agree. The finite corpus also satisfies all 1,771,561 DL triangle
checks, while the explicit OSA triple fails. One thousand seeded repeated/full-
byte pairs, all 256 byte swap cases, signed-char/NUL/UTF-8 controls, actual
last-occurrence/candidate bookkeeping, rectangular/border counts, exact caps,
owned results and malformed local witnesses/instructions are covered. The
nine new cases pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan. These algorithmic controls
need no unrelated downloaded dataset.

> References and reproducibility

- F. J. Damerau (1964), “A Technique for Computer Detection and Correction
  of Spelling Errors,” Communications of the ACM 7(3), pp. 171–176:
  https://doi.org/10.1145/363958.363994 . Its historical single-error correction
  model motivates the operation classes rather than the unrestricted DP.
- R. Lowrance and R. A. Wagner (1975), “An Extension of the String-to-String
  Correction Problem,” Journal of the ACM 22(2), pp. 177–183:
  https://doi.org/10.1145/321879.321880 . This report uses unit costs and the
  full last-occurrence recurrence, with explicit checked clipping and capture.

Every block is a complete editable C++23 program using dm/dm.hpp. The core
supplies distance algorithms, the actual table/candidates, script expansion
and checked edit replay. Native figures are saved locally to build/ and
copied into figures/. Results contain tables and image references, not SVG
XML, data URIs or base64 payloads.
